WEBVTT

00:03.880 --> 00:04.240
All right.

00:04.240 --> 00:08.040
So then we're going to talk about how to optimize the filtering in SQL.

00:08.040 --> 00:13.440
So the tip here is to create a non-clustered index on frequently used columns in Where clause.

00:13.440 --> 00:15.960
So of course you have to check your queries and so on.

00:15.960 --> 00:21.640
And if you see that you are frequently filtering the data using the order status, then it makes sense

00:21.640 --> 00:27.240
to create a non-clustered index for this column in order to improve the performance of your query.

00:27.240 --> 00:32.720
So for this situation, I'm going to go and create then a non-clustered index for the table sales order

00:32.720 --> 00:34.200
for the order status.

00:34.200 --> 00:37.800
So once you create it then you are improving.

00:37.800 --> 00:40.040
Now the performance of your query okay.

00:40.080 --> 00:44.680
The next one is avoid applying functions to columns in the Where clause.

00:44.680 --> 00:49.960
So in many cases what you usually do is that we go and transform the columns before like filtering the

00:49.960 --> 00:50.400
data.

00:50.440 --> 00:55.560
Like for example here I'm applying the function lower on the order status because I'm searching for

00:55.560 --> 00:56.720
the value delivered.

00:56.720 --> 01:02.480
And I'm not sure about the values in the table, whether they have like a camel case or upper case or

01:02.480 --> 01:03.240
anything.

01:03.240 --> 01:05.810
But in order to make sure that I'm going to find the value.

01:05.850 --> 01:10.570
I'm going to go and say lower the order status and then give here a lower value.

01:10.570 --> 01:11.570
And of course it's going to work.

01:11.570 --> 01:16.370
So if I go and search for it and as you can see, we have here the status delivered and the value is

01:16.370 --> 01:17.570
different than the one I used.

01:17.570 --> 01:20.530
Because here we have like a capital first character.

01:20.530 --> 01:22.090
But here we have a problem.

01:22.090 --> 01:24.410
We have an index on the order status.

01:24.450 --> 01:30.170
And now if you use any functions like for example here, the lower the SQL will not use the index.

01:30.450 --> 01:35.290
So that means the whole index is now useless and the SQL is not using it.

01:35.490 --> 01:40.810
And that's why we consider it as a bad practice to use functions for the Where clause.

01:40.850 --> 01:42.730
And instead of that, they're good practice.

01:42.770 --> 01:48.770
Thus, to not use any function and to write exactly the value that is used inside your data.

01:48.770 --> 01:53.130
And with that, the SQL is going to be happy and use the index that you have created.

01:53.170 --> 01:55.490
Okay, let's have another example about this rule.

01:55.490 --> 01:59.450
And here we are selecting all the customers where the first name starts with the A.

01:59.610 --> 02:05.170
So with us we can go and use the function substring in order to get the first character of the first

02:05.210 --> 02:05.530
name.

02:05.530 --> 02:08.210
And once you match it with a, then you will get the result.

02:08.210 --> 02:09.260
And here we have Anna.

02:09.260 --> 02:12.340
And this is again bad if you have an index on the first name.

02:12.340 --> 02:15.700
And that's because we are applying a function on the column.

02:15.700 --> 02:17.820
So this is considered to be a bad practice.

02:17.820 --> 02:21.140
And instead of that we can go and use the help of the like.

02:21.300 --> 02:24.140
So we can go and search for this pattern where it starts with the A.

02:24.140 --> 02:25.660
And then we have a white card.

02:25.860 --> 02:27.140
We don't care about the rest.

02:27.140 --> 02:28.860
So it must start with a.

02:29.100 --> 02:31.940
So if we go and execute it you will get the same results.

02:31.940 --> 02:37.860
So try as much as you can to avoid the functions in the Where clause in order to hit and get the index

02:37.860 --> 02:38.500
working.

02:38.500 --> 02:43.620
And in many scenarios we have a workaround in order to use the function without transformations.

02:43.620 --> 02:48.100
So try your best to avoid using functions if your columns having an index.

02:48.140 --> 02:53.180
All right, one more example that you see a lot on queries that you filter by the year.

02:53.180 --> 02:56.700
So we are searching for the orders that happens in 2025.

02:56.740 --> 02:59.620
And we usually go and use the year order dates.

02:59.660 --> 03:04.620
And now if you have an index on the order dates, this again will not be working because you are using

03:04.620 --> 03:05.620
a function here.

03:05.660 --> 03:08.060
So this considered to be a bad practice.

03:08.220 --> 03:11.820
Instead of using the year function, you can go and use between.

03:11.820 --> 03:17.260
So we don't apply a function on the order date, and we say the order date is between the boundaries

03:17.260 --> 03:18.020
of the year.

03:18.060 --> 03:23.380
Of course, now our query is not looking really cool and easy like the first one, but still with the

03:23.380 --> 03:25.420
second one we are hitting the index.

03:25.500 --> 03:31.340
So again, while you are filtering, try to not use functions on the columns because it is really waste.

03:31.340 --> 03:36.020
If you have an index and you are not using it, and most of the cases you have like a workaround for

03:36.020 --> 03:36.820
your function.

03:36.820 --> 03:40.140
So those are the three examples that I wanted to show you about this tip.

03:40.180 --> 03:40.500
All right.

03:40.500 --> 03:41.700
Moving on to a similar one.

03:41.740 --> 03:46.180
It says avoid leading wildcards as they prevent index usage.

03:46.260 --> 03:47.460
So this is a similar one.

03:47.460 --> 03:51.740
Let's say for example I'm searching for the word gold inside the last name.

03:51.740 --> 03:54.260
And here we have to be careful what we are searching for.

03:54.420 --> 04:01.420
Should the gold exist somewhere in the last name, or only we are searching for the last name that start

04:01.420 --> 04:02.300
with gold.

04:02.300 --> 04:05.980
If it's like that, we are searching only the last name that starts with gold.

04:06.020 --> 04:07.620
Then we are doing it here wrong.

04:07.660 --> 04:13.380
And in SQL, if you are using the leading wildcards, then the SQL will not be using the index.

04:13.380 --> 04:16.590
But if you are using the wildcard at the end and the trailing.

04:16.630 --> 04:19.910
This one is fine and skill will not avoid using the index.

04:19.910 --> 04:26.190
So this considered as a bad practice because you will not be hitting the index better than that to not

04:26.190 --> 04:28.150
use the white card as a leading.

04:28.190 --> 04:33.510
And if that's enough for your search, then with that you are hitting and using the index.

04:33.550 --> 04:34.910
Okay, moving on to the next one.

04:34.910 --> 04:38.470
It says use in instead of multiple or or.

04:38.510 --> 04:42.750
Operator is very evil for performance and try to avoid using it.

04:42.790 --> 04:46.990
It really kills your performance, whether it is in the filters or joints and so on.

04:46.990 --> 04:51.590
So now we want to show the orders where the customers is equal to one or 2 or 3.

04:51.630 --> 04:55.830
And of course this is considered to be a bad practice and hard to read and so on.

04:55.830 --> 04:56.950
Please don't do that.

04:56.990 --> 05:03.350
Instead we have the in operator and we are saying if the customer is one of those values, then show

05:03.350 --> 05:04.110
the orders.

05:04.110 --> 05:07.310
So if you go and run it, you will get the exact results.

05:07.310 --> 05:12.830
And it's not only looks nicer than the first query, but it has as well a better performance.

05:12.830 --> 05:17.030
So if you find out writing a lot of orders, think about the in operator.

05:17.030 --> 05:21.150
So those are the best practices for filtering data to improve the performance.
